# Untitled

Enhanced Intermolecular Electron Transfer in Fluorinated Metal–Organic Framework Photocatalysts for Efficient \(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\) Reduction
Efficient electron transfer from photosensitizer to catalytic sites is crucial for effective artificial photosynthesis, yet it remains a significant challenge. Herein, it is reported that simple fluorination of the organic linkers in the MIL-101(Fe) photocatalyst results in a remarkable threefold increase in the photocatalytic \(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\)-to-\(\mathbf{CO}\) conversion rate \((688\,\upmu\mathrm{mol}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\,\mathrm{h}^{-1})\) compared to the pristine counterpart \((230\,\upmu\mathrm{mol}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\,\mathrm{h}^{-1})\). It is unveiled that, instead of directly modifying the electron structure of MIL-101(Fe), the fluorinated linkers enhance the interaction between the discrete photocatalyst and photosensitizer \(\left(\left[\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}\right]^{2+}\right.\), \(\left.\mathrm{bpy}=2,2^{\prime}\right.\)-bipyridine) via hydrogen bonding, thereby facilitating their intermolecular electron transfer. Most importantly, it is also demonstrated that this performance boosting strategy can be applied to other Fe-based metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) photocatalysts such as MIL-53(Fe) and MIL-88(Fe). The present work not only underscores the fluorination of organic linkers as a generic promising approach to enhance the photocatalytic performance of MOF-based catalysts, but also holds significant implications for photosynthesis and catalytic processes reliant on intermolecular electron transfer as an important step.
## 1. Introduction
The excessive emission of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) from the consumption of fossil fuels has caused a severe energy crisis and environmental pollution issues, which greatly endanger the sustainable development of human beings and thus need to be tackled urgently.[1] The photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction reaction \(\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\right)\) offers a promising avenue for converting \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) into valuable chemicals and/or fuels, thereby effectively neutralizing \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) emissions and closing the carbon cycle.[2,3] However, the techno-economic feasibility of this solar-driven \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) valorization route is largely challenged by the limited efficiency of photocatalysts. A high-performance photocatalytic system toward \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction needs to efficiently address the following three steps. 1) Light absorption: a photosensitizer molecule absorbs a photon, leading to the excitation of an electron to a higher energy level; 2) Electron transfer: the excited electron is transferred from the photosensitizer
to the catalytic sites; 3) Catalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction: the electrons at the catalytic sites reduce \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), driving the reaction forward. Over the past few decades, significant progress has been made in the development of photosensitizers with high light absorption coefficients (e.g., \(\left[\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}\right]^{2+}\), \(\mathrm{phen}=1,10\)-phenanthroline; \(\left[\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}\right]^{2+}\), \(\mathrm{bpy}=2,2^{\prime}\)-bipyridine) and highly active photocatalysts for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption and activation (e.g., inorganic semiconductors,[4,5] metal-incorporated zeolites,[6,7] and metal complexes[8,9]). However, the slow electron transfer between discrete photosensitizers and catalytic sites has impeded the utilization of photoexcited electrons, resulting in low overall photocatalytic efficiency.[10] One proven strategy to alleviate this issue is to integrate the photosensitizer and catalytically active sites into a single system.[11] For instance, Fischer et al. demonstrated that entrapping a molecular photocatalyst \(\left(\left[\mathrm{ReBr}(\mathrm{CO})_{3}(4,4\mathrm{-}\mathrm{dcbpy})\right]\right.\), \(\left.\mathrm{dcbpy}=\mathrm{dicarboxy}-2,2^{\prime}\right.\)-bipyridine) and a photosensitizer \(\left(\left[\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{2}(5,5^{\prime}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{dcbpy})\right]^{2+}\right)\) within the same cage of a MIL-101(Cr) leads to an improved photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\)-to-\(\mathrm{CO}\) conversion rate, facilitated by accelerated intermolecular electron transfer.[12] Cao et al. showed that integrating a photosensitizer \(\left(\left[\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}\right]^{2+}\right)\) and catalytic sites (cobalt porphyrin)
into a covalent organic framework promoted through bond intermolecular electron transfer, resulting in a 1.4 times higher photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\)-to-CO conversion rate than a physical mixture of the photosensitizer and photocatalyst.[10] Nevertheless, the construction of such sophisticated photocatalytic systems remains greatly challenging, thus impeding their scaled-up synthesis. Additionally, efforts to anchor or entrap homogeneous photosensitizer molecules onto solid supporting materials or photocatalysts can compromise the reactivity of the photosensitizers due to restricted mobility.[13] Therefore, exploring alternative approaches to achieving more efficient charge transfer between the photosensitizer and photocatalyst is highly desired.
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are a novel class of highly ordered porous materials formed by assembling metal ions or clusters with organic linkers through coordination.[14] Benefiting from their unique features, such as isolated active sites, large specific surface area, high porosity, and tailorable structure and functionality, MOFs have garnered significant attention for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture and photocatalytic conversion.[11,15–18] The catalytic performance of MOFs can be optimized by modifying their topological structure and coordination environment,[11,19] through approaches such as metal node doping,[20–22] linker functionalization/substitutions,[23–26] and active guests encapsulation.[17,27,28] Among these strategies, the incorporation of linkers containing task-specific functional groups into MOFs represents one of the most facile and effective means to tailor their photocatalytic properties while preserving material crystallinity and porosity.[29,30] However, a consensus understanding of how these functional groups impact the electronic/geometric structure, electron transfer properties, and ultimately the photocatalytic performance of MOFs remains elusive.
The objective of this study is to present a universal approach for enhancing the efficiency of electron transfer between a photosensitizer and MOF-based photocatalyst using ligand structure engineering. As a proof of concept, we selected the well-known and cost-effective Fe-MOF photocatalyst, MIL-101(Fe), and a homogeneous photosensitizer, \([\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}]^{2+}\), as a model system. The choice was motivated by the broad range of functionalized ligands available for MIL-101(Fe)[31,32] and the high light absorption coefficient of the \([\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}]^{2+}\) complex.[33] We showed that the incorporation of fluorinated linkers, such as 2-fluoroterephthalic acid \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC-F}\right)\) and 2,5-difluoroterephthalic acid \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC-F},\mathrm{F}\right)\), into MIL-101(Fe) significantly boosts the photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\)-to-CO conversion rate up to \(688\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}\), which is three times higher than that of pristine counterpart \((230\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1})\). Combining various advanced experimental characterizations and theoretical calculations, we unveiled that the introduction of fluorinated linkers strengthens the interaction between the photocatalyst and photosensitizer via hydrogen bonding, thereby promoting intermolecular electron transfer and resulting in enhanced photocatalytic activity for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction. Moreover, we showed that this generic strategy has successfully been applied to other two Fe-MOFs, i.e., MIL-53(Fe) and MIL-88(Fe) systems. These findings provide a new perspective for developing high-performance photocatalysts through molecular structure engineering.
## 2. Results
## 2.1. Synthesis and Characterization of MIL-101(Fe)
The pristine MIL-101(Fe) was synthesized via a simple solvothermal reaction between \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{Cl})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\) in an \(\mathrm{N},\mathrm{N}^{\prime}\)-dimethylformamide (DMF) solution.[34] To obtain partially fluorinated MIL-101(Fe) with fluorinated linkers, namely "xMIL-101-F" or "xMIL-101-F,F" (where x represents the molar ratio of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC-F}\) or \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC-F},\mathrm{F}\) to \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\)), a mixed-ligand strategy was employed under the same reaction conditions (Scheme 1).
The morphologies and chemical compositions of the as-prepared materials were first characterized by scanning electron microscopy (SEM), high-angle annular dark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM), and energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) elemental mapping. As shown in Figure 1, all materials exhibit characteristic octahedral morphologies of MIL-101, and the corresponding averaged particle sizes are \(1.5\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\), \(600\,\mathrm{nm}\), and \(400\,\mathrm{nm}\) for MIL-101, 0.25MIL-101-F, and 0.25MIL-101-F,F, respectively. The EDS elemental mapping analysis further confirms the homogeneous dispersion of C, O, Fe, and F throughout the particles of 0.25MIL-101-F and 0.25MIL-101-F,F. These results indicate the successful and uniform incorporation of fluorinated ligands \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC-F}\right.\) or \(\left.\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC-F},\mathrm{F}\right)\) into MIL-101(Fe).
The X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of the as-prepared materials are presented in Figure 2a. All diffraction peaks closely match the simulated pattern of MIL-101(Fe), indicating the structure of MIL-101 is well preserved after the introduction of fluorinated linkers. Notably, the characteristic diffraction peaks of 0.25MIL-101-F and 0.25MIL-101-F,F slightly shifted to lower diffraction angles compared to pristine MIL-101, suggesting a minor lattice expansion due to the incorporation of fluorinated linkers.[35] Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectroscopy was also performed to analyze the molecular structures. The functional groups of the as-prepared pristine and partially fluorinated MIL-101 were identified. As depicted in Figure 2b, the obtained spectra exhibit characteristic IR absorption peaks at \(\approx1612\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) (asymmetric vibrations of \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\)), \(1508\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) (aromatic framework vibrations), \(1387\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) (symmetric vibrations of \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\)), \(1020\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) (stretching vibrations of \(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{O}\)), \(745\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) (out-of-plane bending vibrations of \(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{H}\) in the benzene rings), and \(552\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) (stretching vibrations of \(\mathrm{Fe}-\mathrm{O}\)), consistent with previously reported MIL-101(Fe) structures.[36–38] Additionally, a broad peak emerges \(\approx1060\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) in both 0.25MIL-101-F and 0.25MIL-101-F,F, which can be attributed to C-F vibrations.[39] These results verify the successful incorporation of fluorinated linker into the MIL-101(Fe) framework.
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was then employed to examine the surface chemical composition and electronic structure of pristine MIL-101, 0.25MIL-101-F, and 0.25MIL-101-F,F. The XPS survey spectra reveal characteristic signals of C 1s, O 1s, and Fe 2p (Figure 2c). Notably, the spectra of 0.25MIL-101-F and 0.25MIL-101-F,F exhibit distinct peaks corresponding to F 1s, confirming the presence of fluorine groups. The high-resolution C 1s spectra of pristine MIL-101 show three peaks at \(\approx288.6\), 286.4, and \(284.8\,\mathrm{eV}\) (Figure 2d), corresponding to \(\mathrm{O}-\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\), \(\mathrm{O}-\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{O}\), and \(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{C}\), respectively.[40,41] The emergence of an additional peak at \(287.1\,\mathrm{eV}\) on the fluorinated samples can
be regarded as a signature of the C–F bonds,[40] further confirming the successful incorporation of fluorinated ligands. The high-resolution XPS spectra of Fe 2p (Figure 2e) exhibit predominant peaks at \(711.3~\mathrm{eV}\) (Fe \(2{\tt p}_{1/2}\)) and \(724.4~\mathrm{eV}\) (Fe \(2{\tt p}_{1/2}\)), accompanied by satellite signals at \([218.9\) and \(731.8\,\mathrm{eV}\), respectively. Semiquantitative analysis demonstrates that 0.25MIL-101-F, F exhibits a higher surface atomic percentage of fluorine (F) at \(3.66\%\) compared to 0.125MIL-101-F-F \((1.40\%)\) as expected (Table S1, Supporting Information). Furthermore, increasing the molar concentration of the fluorinated linker during synthesis can result in a higher surface atomic percentage of F, reaching up to \(4.30\%\).
Importantly, the Fe 2p signals of 0.25MIL-101-F and 0.25MIL-101-F, F closely resemble those of the pristine counterpart, with minimal changes in the major peak positions, suggesting a negligible influence on the surface electronic structure of Fe upon incorporation of fluorinated linkers. Similar observations are made in the XPS spectra of O 1s, where minimal changes are observed following the introduction of fluorinated linkers (Figure S1, Supporting Information). These results strongly suggest that despite the strong electron with-drawing capability of the fluorine group, its perturbation to the electronic structure is likely limited to the benzene ring, rather than extending to adjacent carboxylic
groups or Fe−O clusters in MIL-101(Fe). Figure 2f shows the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) -sorption isotherms of MIL-101, 0.25MIL-101-F, and 0.25MIL101-F,F, all displaying a typical type-I isotherm curve with a distinct secondary uptake in the relative pressure range of 0.1–0.2, attributed to micropore filling.[42] The BET surface area values were determined to be 3389, 2937, and \(2821\,\mathrm{~m}^{2}\,\mathrm{~g}^{-1}\) for MIL101, 0.25MIL-101-F, and 0.25MIL-101-F,F, respectively, indicating that the porous structure of MIL-101 remains largely intact upon the introduction of fluorinated ligands. The slight decrease in BET surface area for 0.25MIL-101-F and 0.25MIL-101-F,F compared to the pristine counterpart can be attributed to the occupied volume of the fluorine groups and/or slight lattice distortion, as observed in the XRD patterns.[42]
To investigate the potential influence of fluorinated groups on the local coordination structure of Fe species in MIL-101(Fe), the synchrotron-based X-ray near-edge structure (XANES) and extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) measurements were conducted. The XANES curves of MIL-101, 0.25MIL-101-F, 0.25MIL-101-F,F, as well as reference samples including Fe foil, FeO, and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) , are presented in Figure 3. Remarkably, the nearedge absorption energy of MIL-101(Fe) based samples closely resembles that of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) , implying the prevalence of Fe(III) within MIL-101(Fe) in accordance with the XPS results. Furthermore, the near-edge curves of the fluorinated MIL-101(Fe) overlap perfectly with that of the pristine counterpart (Figure 3a), providing compelling evidence that the incorporation of a fluorinated linker does not alter the electronic structure of Fe in MIL-101(Fe). The Fourier-transformed EXAFS curves (Figure 3b), reveal insights into the radial distribution of nearest neighbor atoms surround
ing Fe. For the MIL-101(Fe) based samples, a prominent peak at \({\approx}1.5\mathrm{~\AA~}\) , corresponding to Fe−O bonds, is the sole discernible feature, while the peak indicative of Fe−Fe bonds observed in the Fe foil at \(2.2\textup{\AA}\) is absent. This observation suggests the absence of Fe or FeO particles throughout the MIL-101(Fe) samples, regardless of the presence of fluorinated linkers. To further validate the atomic dispersion of iron species, wavelet transform (WT) analysis of the EXAFS curves was recorded considering its powerful resolutions in both \(k\) and \(R\) spaces.[43] The WT contour plots of the MIL-101(Fe)-based samples (Figure 3c) demonstrate a single intensity maximum at \({\approx}5\mathrm{~\AA^{-1}}\) , corresponding to the Fe−O bonds,[44] thus corroborating the presence of atomically dispersed iron species within MIL-101(Fe). Overall, these results clearly demonstrate that both the geometric and surface electronic structure of MIL-101(Fe) are well-maintained after the introduction of fluorinated linkers, which provides a solid foundation for elucidating the impact of additional functional groups on the critical charge transfer efficiency during photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction.
## 2.2. Photocatalytic \(\mathbf{co}_{2}\) Reduction Performance
The effects of fluorinated ligands on the performance of photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction reaction \((\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR})\) were carried out in a \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) - saturated \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}\) solution containing triethanolamine (TEOA) at 1 atm under irradiation from a 300 W Xe lamp with a \(400\,\mathrm{nm}\) filter. Gas chromatography (GC) and proton nuclear magnetic resonance ( \(\mathrm{^{1}H}\) -NMR) analysis confirmed CO as the sole product of
the reaction. While a pioneering study reported that MIL-101(Fe) could convert \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to formate,[45] there are numerous recent studies documenting that CO is the primary product over MIL101(Fe), MIL-53(Fe), MIL-88B(Fe), Fe complex and Fe-O clusters with or without the usage of photosensitizer,[46–51] aligning with our current findings.
The photocatalytic activity was examined by performing the photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction under visible light irradiation for \(6\,\mathrm{~h~}\) . It can be seen that the amount of produced CO increased steadily with the irradiation time for all the investigated catalysts (Figure S2, Supporting Information). Notably, 0.25MIL-101-F-F and 0.25MIL-101-F exhibited significantly enhanced photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) performance, yielding rates of \(688~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) (yield: \(4128\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\)) and \(645~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) (yield: \(3870\,\upmu\mathrm{mol}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\)), respectively, compared to the pristine MIL101 \((230\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\;\mathrm{h}^{-1}\), yield: \(1380\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\)) (Figure 4a). These results demonstrate a threefold and 2.8-fold increase in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) efficiency for 0.25MIL-101-F,F, and 0.25MIL-101-F, respectively. Single-variable experiments were conducted to establish the essential components for the photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\), revealing the necessity of TEOA, photosensitizer, catalyst, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), and light. Importantly, when \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) was replaced with Ar, no CO production was observed in the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) (Figure 4a), indicating that CO originates from \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and not from the catalyst or photosensitizer de
composition. To elucidate the catalytic role of MIL-101(Fe) in the photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction, we performed electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) measurements under catalytic conditions with 0.25MIL-101-F,F, photosensitizer, and TEOA in acetonitrile. Figure S3 (Supporting Information) shows the characteristic EPR signal of \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) in the octahedral \(\mathrm{FeO}_{6}\) sites of MIL-101(Fe) under dark conditions. Upon visible light irradiation, the EPR signal was almost completely suppressed, indicating the reduction of \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) to \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\). When \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) was introduced into the system, the EPR signal partially recovered, suggesting the re-oxidation of \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) to \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\). These results confirm that the iron centers within the MIL101 framework act as the active sites for photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction.
The concentration of fluorinated ligands in MIL-101 can be modulated by varying the added amount of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}-\mathrm{F}\) or \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}-\mathrm{F},\mathrm{F}\). A series of xMIL-101-F \((x=0.125\), 0.25, and 0.5) and xMIL-101-F,F \((x=0.125\), 0.25, and 0.5) photocatalysts were prepared, incorporating different molar ratios of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}-\mathrm{F}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}-\mathrm{F},\mathrm{F}-\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\). In addition, FeBDC-F and FeBDC-F,F were synthesized through the solvothermal reaction between \(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}\cdot6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}-\mathrm{F}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}-\mathrm{F},\mathrm{F}\), without the presence of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\) in a DMF solution The original octahedron-like morphology of MIL101 was maintained in these prepared samples, as evidenced by TEM, SEM, HAADF-STEM, and EDS elemental mapping images
(Figures S4–S7, Supporting Information). The chemical composition and high crystallinity of these samples were confirmed by XRD patterns and XPS spectra (Figures S8 and S9, Supporting Information), which exhibited isostructural characteristics with MIL-101.
An attempt was made to explore the impact of photosensitizer concentration on the photocatalytic activity of both MIL-101(Fe) and 0.25MIL-101-F, F by varying the dosage of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}\) from 25 to \(100~\mathrm{mg}\) . The initial observation reveals that the photocatalytic activity, measured in terms of CO production rate, depends sensitively on the photosensitizer dosage, with the highest activity achieved at a dosage of \(50~\mathrm{mg}\) for both catalysts (Figure S12, Supporting Information). This unique dependence phenomenon can be rationalized by the trade-off between enhanced light ab
sorption and reduced light penetration with increasing photosensitizer dosage. Furthermore, it is noteworthy that even with a lower photosensitizer dosage, 0.25MIL-101-F, F exhibits substantially higher photocatalytic activity than the pristine counterpart, emphasizing the significance of the fluorinated linker in determining photocatalytic activity.
Moreover, the reusability and stability of 0.25MIL-101-F, F were assessed by five consecutive \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) experiments under identical reaction conditions. No observable changes in morphology, crystalline structure, or photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction performance were detected for the 0.25MIL-101-F, F after five consecutive runs (Figure 4d; Figure S13, Supporting Information). This indicates the excellent stability of 0.25MIL-101-F, F in the context of photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) .
To illustrate the broad applicability of fluorinated ligand engineering in Fe-MOFs for photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) , we explored the photocatalytic performance of other Fe-containing MOF materials. Specifically, MIL-53(Fe) and MIL-88(Fe) were chosen due to their similar \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\) linkers to MIL-101(Fe), albeit with distinct topologies.[45] The successful construction of MIL-53(Fe) and MIL-88(Fe) was initially confirmed by their characteristic XRD patterns, which closely matched those of the simulated high-temperature MIL-53 (MIL-53ht) and MIL-88B, respectively (Figure S14, Supporting Information). It is noteworthy that the incorporation of fluorinated linkers had minimal impact on both the morphology and crystalline structure. This was evidenced by SEM, HAADF-STEM, and EDS elemental mapping and XRD characterizations of MIL-53, 0.25MIL-53-F,F, MIL-88, and 0.25MIL-88-F,F (Figures S14–S16, Supporting Information). The photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction performance of MIL-53, MIL-88, 0.25MIL-53-F,F, and 0.25MIL-88-F,F is summarized in
Figure 4a. Notably, the introduction of fluorinated ligands significantly enhanced the photocatalytic activity, leading to increased CO production rates of \(162\,\upmu\mathrm{mol}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\,\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) for MIL-53 and \(196\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}\) for MIL-88, while reaching \(407\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}\) for 0.25MIL-53-F, F and \(436\,\upmu\mathrm{mol}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\,\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) for 0.25MIL-88-F, F, respectively. These results further demonstrate the universal applicability of the ligand fluorination strategy in promoting the photocatalytic performance of Fe-MOFs.
## 2.3. Electron Transfer Kinetics
Given the pivotal role of electron transfer processes in determining photocatalytic efficiency, the present work aims to investigate the effects of fluorinated linkers on the electron transfer kinetics of MIL-101(Fe) in the presence of photosensitizers. To assess the electron transfer efficiency, EPR spectroscopy was employed, using the photocatalytic production of superoxide radicals \((\bullet\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-})\) as a probe reaction. This approach was chosen due to the facile reduction of \(\mathrm{O}_{x}\) by photogenerated electrons, leading to the formation of \(\bullet\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}\) radical.[52] The quantification of \(\bullet\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}\) was achieved using EPR analysis with 5,5-dimethyl-l-pyrroline N-oxide (DMPO) as a trapping agent. As depicted in Figure 5a,b, no EPR signals were observed under dark conditions \((t=0\ \mathrm{s})\) for both samples. In contrast, clear EPR signals corresponding to DMPO- \(\bullet\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}\) radicals were observed for the pristine MIL-101 and 0.25MIL-101-F under visible-light irradiation. Notably, 0.25MIL-101-F exhibited four distinct peaks in contrast to the weak EPR signals of the pristine MIL-101, indicating an enhanced reduction capability of the fluorinated sample due to faster photoelectron transfer kinetics. The \(\bullet\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}\) generation rates over different samples were summarized in Figure 5c, determined through the slope analysis of the EPR signal intensity plotted against irradiation time. It can be observed that \(\bullet\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}\) generation rate \((1.35\ \mathrm{s}^{-1})\) for 0.25MIL-101-F was significantly faster than that of the pristine MIL-101 \((0.62\ \mathrm{s}^{-1})\).
tron transfer kinetics. The \(\bullet\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}\) generation rates over different samples were summarized in Figure 5c, determined through the slope analysis of the EPR signal intensity plotted against irradiation time. It can be observed that \(\bullet\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}\) generation rate \((1.35\ \mathrm{s}^{-1})\) for 0.25MIL-101-F was significantly faster than that of the pristine MIL-101 \((0.62\ \mathrm{s}^{-1})\) .
To elucidate the photogenerated carrier transfer and separation efficiency, the steady-state photoluminescence (PL) spectra were measured under excitation at \(360\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Figure 5d). Remarkably, the peak intensity of 0.25MIL-101-F was substantially reduced compared to the pristine MIL-101. This suggests that the recombination rate of electrons and holes in 0.25MIL-101-F is effectively suppressed. Moreover, transient-state PL spectra results corroborated a longer average charge carrier lifetime for 0.25MIL-101-F (5.84 ns) compared to the pristine MIL-101 (3.09 ns) (Figure 5e,f; Table S4, Supporting Information). Additionally, we investigated the impact of a fluorinated linker on the charge transfer efficiency between the photosensitizer \(\left(\left[\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}\right]^{2+}\right)\) and the photocatalyst (MIL-101(Fe) or 0.25MIL-101-F, F) by collecting PL spectra (Figure S17a, Supporting Information). Evidently, the PL intensity of \(\left[\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}\right]^{2+}\) significantly decreased in the presence of photocatalysts due to enhanced electron transfer and subsequent electron-hole recombination suppression. Notably, 0.25MIL-101-F more effectively reduced the PL intensity of \(\left[\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}\right]^{2+}\) compared to MIL-101(Fe), indicating that the fluorinated linker enhances intermolecular electron transfer kinetics due to a stronger interaction between the photosensitizer and photocatalyst. Furthermore, the transient PL spectra and corresponding fitting parameters of the pristine photosensitizer, and its mixture with MIL-101 and 0.25MIL-101-F, F are
presented in Figure S17 (Supporting Information). The pristine photosensitizer exhibits an average lifetime of \( \approx 494.5 \) ns. In contrast, the MIL-101 and 0.25MIL-101-F,F samples reveal a notable reduction in the average lifetime of the photosensitizer, with values of 405.4 and 373.4 ns, respectively. This reduction provides clear evidence that the excited electrons from the photosensitizer can more effectively migrate to the surface of 0.25MIL-101-F,F. These findings demonstrate conclusively that the incorporation of fluorinated ligands promotes the efficient transfer of photogenerated electrons from the photosensitizer to the photocatalyst, contributing to favorable photocatalytic CO2RR.
## 3. Discussion
The aforementioned findings provide compelling evidence that the incorporation of fluorinated linkers boosts the photocatalytic activity of Fe-MOFs in \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) reduction, which is largely attributed to the facilitated electron transfer process, as supported by EPR and steady/transient-state PL analysis. Nevertheless, the mechanism for how the fluorinated linkers promote electron transfer kinetics and boost photocatalytic \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) reduction activity over Fe-MOFs remains elusive.
It is noteworthy that the inclusion of fluorinated linkers has induced a significant alteration in the particle size of MIL-101(Fe), decreasing from \( 1.5 \, \upmu \mathrm{m} \) for the pristine MIL-101(Fe) to 600 and \( 400 \, \mathrm{nm} \) for 0.25MIL-101-F and 0.25MIL-101-F,F, respectively. To clarify whether the particle size influences the photocatalytic performance of MIL-101(Fe), an attempt was made to tune the particle sizes by adjusting the concentration of \( \mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{BCD} \) in DMF solution, resulting in the successful formation of MIL-101(Fe) particles with average sizes of 600 and \( 400 \, \mathrm{nm} \) (Figure S18, Supporting Information). As shown in Figure 6a, all three samples exhibited comparable photocatalytic activity, with values of 224, 242, and \( 230 \, \upmu \mathrm{mol} \, \mathrm{g}^{-1} \, \mathrm{h}^{-1} \) for MIL-101 with the particle sizes of \( 400 \, \mathrm{nm} \), \( 600 \, \mathrm{nm} \), and \( 1.5 \, \upmu \mathrm{m} \), respectively. This observation confirms that the photocatalytic activity of MIL-101 is insensitive to particle sizes within the particle range, which can partially be attributed to the relatively large particle sizes. The textural and crystalline structure of large MIL-101(Fe) crystals would remain consistent across samples of different sizes, making them more or less comparable to the properties of bulk materials.
Given that the \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) uptake capability of a photocatalyst can influence the local concentration of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) molecules at catalytic sites,
the \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) affinities of the Fe-MOFs were measured. \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) sorption experiments were performed at two temperatures (273 and 298 K) on MIL-101, 0.25MIL-101-F, and 0.25MIL-101-F,F (Figure S19, Supporting Information).[53] Intriguingly, all materials exhibited similar \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) uptake values \( (37.8–39.5 \, \mathrm{cm}^{3} \, \mathrm{g}^{-1} \) at \( 273 \, \mathrm{K} \), \( 22.2–22.4 \, \mathrm{cm}^{3} \, \mathrm{g}^{-1} \) at \( 298 \, \mathrm{K} \)) and isosteric heat of adsorption \( (Q_{\mathrm{st}}) \) values \( (30.3–33.1 \, \mathrm{kJ} \, \mathrm{mol}^{-1}) \) (Figure 6b). These results indicate that the enhancement in photocatalytic performance through fluorinated linkers is unlikely to be mediated by alterations in \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) uptake capability.
To determine the impact of a fluorinated linker in MIL-101(Fe) on TEOA adsorption, we conducted contact angle measurements by placing drops of TEOA solution on both the catalyst layer of MIL-101(Fe) and 0.25 MIL-101-F,F. Figure S20 (Supporting Information) illustrates the contact angles of TEOA on both samples. Initially, the contact angles are similar, but the stabilized contact angle on fluorinated MIL-101(Fe) is slightly higher than that on pristine MIL-101(Fe). This suggests that the presence of a fluorinated linker may weaken the molecular interaction between MIL-101(Fe) and TEOA. This phenomenon can be explained by the fact that adding a fluorine atom to a molecule generally increases its hydrophobicity because fluorine atoms are less polarizable than hydrogen atoms. Consequently, fluorinated molecules are expected to have weaker interactions with polar molecules like water and TEOA. These results indicate that TEOA adsorption on MIL-101(Fe) is hindered by the presence of the fluorinated group, suggesting that it is unlikely to significantly promote photocatalytic \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) reduction.
While the presence of highly efficient photosensitizers diminishes the role of the photocatalyst in affecting photoresponse properties, the band structure of photocatalysts can affect the electron transfer behavior between the photosensitizer and photocatalyst. Therefore, the bandgaps and conduction-band (CB) positions of MIL-101, 0.25MIL-101-F, and 0.25MIL-101-F,F were determined using UV–vis diffuse reflectance spectra (UV–vis DRS) and the Mott–Schottky (M–S) analysis. It turns out that both the bandgaps and CB positions remained comparable regardless of the presence of fluorinated linkers. Specifically, the bandgaps were measured to be 2.57, 2.60, and \( 2.58 \, \mathrm{eV} \), while the CB positions were determined as \( -1.05 \), \( -1.08 \) and \( -1.02 \, \mathrm{eV} \) for MIL-101, 0.25MIL-101-F and 0.25MIL-101-F,F, respectively (Figure 6c; Figures S21–S23, Supporting Information). These findings exclude the possibility of fluorinated ligands exerting an
effect through modification of these structural parameters of FeMOFs.
A number of previous studies have already explored the influence of ligands on the electronic structure of metal centers in MOFs with various applications.[54–60] For instance, Duan et al. demonstrated that the introduction of an electron-withdrawing group (-F) led to a slight positive charge on Cu sites, favoring the electrocatalytic reduction of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\).[60] Zhu et al. also reported the regulation of the electronic structure of iron centers in MIL-101(Fe) by introducing electron-withdrawing \(\left(-\mathrm{NO}_{2}\right)\) or electron-donating \(\left(-\mathrm{NH}_{3}\right)\) groups on the ligands.[26] These insightful studies have raised the hypothesis that fluorinated linkers could directly modify the electronic structure of catalytically active sites, in particular, the iron centers, within MIL-101(Fe). However, both XPS and XANES analysis (Figures 2e and 3) indicate minimal perturbation of the electronic structure of iron sites upon the introduction of either \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC-F}\) or H2BDC-F,F. Furthermore, despite the different fluorine contents in 0.25MIL101-F and 0.25MIL-101-F,F, these two catalysts exhibit comparable photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) -to-CO conversion rates (645 versus \(688\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1})\) , suggesting that the fluorinated linkers are unlikely to exert a significant effect by modifying the electronic structure of catalytic active sites. To gain more insights into the potential ligand effect of fluorinated linkers on the electronic structure of iron centers in MIL-101, the charge distribution of MIL-101(Fe) with and without fluorinated linkers was calculated (Figures S24 and S25, Supporting Information). It can be seen that the charge distribution over Fe sites shows little change upon the introduction of fluorinated linkers. Although the presence of the fluorine group does cause some perturbation in the charge distribution of adjacent carbon atoms, this effect is mainly confined within the benzene ring. These results once again confirm the negligible ligand effect on the electronic structure of iron active sites.
It is well documented that the spatial orientation of electron-donor and electron-acceptor molecules plays an essential role in determining the through-space electron transfer efficiency,[61–63] which leads us to the hypothesis that the presence of fluorinated linker may promote the intermolecular electron transfer between photocatalyst and photosensitizer by influencing their geometric arrangement. In view of this, we calculated the interaction energy between MIL-101(Fe) and photosensitizer \([\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}]^{2+}\) complex. It has been found that the interaction energy between the photocatalyst and photosensitizer substantially increases from \(-147.2\) to \(-197.8\;\mathrm{kJ}\;\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\) upon the introduction of fluorinated linkers. A closer examination of their molecular structure and orientation of the MIL-101(Fe) and photosensitizer \([\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}]^{2+}\) complex shows that hydrogen atoms in the bipyridine rings of the photosensitizer tend to form the hydrogen bonding with fluorine groups in the linkers. Further analysis of the optimized complex structure reveals a decrease of \({\approx}0.17\mathrm{~\AA~}\) in the closest distance between the H atom of the Ru complex and the F atom of the MOF in the complex, which clearly demonstrates that there is a hydrogen bonding between them. Interestingly, due to the coplanar structure of the bipyridine units in the \([\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}]^{2+}\) complex, the fluorine group can also engage in bidentate hydrogen bonding with two hydrogen atoms (Figure S26, Supporting Information).[64] Due to this unique bidentate hydrogen bonding, the structure of \([\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}]^{2+}\) photosensitizer is free from rotation, effectively fixing its position at an optimal distance from the photocatalyst, and facilitating subsequent intermolecular electron transfer processes.[65] This hypothesis aligns with our experimental findings, where the introduction of one or two fluorine groups onto the BDC linker results in minimal differences in photocatalytic activity. This is due to the fact that only one fluorine group can participate in hydrogen bonding with the Ru complex, while the other is located opposite the BDC ring.
