The findings presented in the present work provide novel insights into the role of fluorinated linkers in enhancing the photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) over Fe-MOFs. Figure 7 illustrates the proposed reaction pathway over the fluorinated MIL-101(Fe) photocatalyst paired with \([\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}]^{2+}\) complex photosensitizer. The first step involves the excitation of the photosensitizer upon light absorption, initiating a series of subsequent electron transfer processes, including the transfer of one electron from the metal center to the ligand (referred to as triplet metal-ligand charge transfer, \(^3\mathrm{MLCT}\)), reductive quenching of the excited photosensitizer by the sacrificial reagent (TEOA), and the formation of the reduced form of the photosensitizer.[66,67] In the second step, electron transfer occurs from the reduced form of the photosensitizer to the neighboring photocatalyst, which significantly influences the photocatalytic efficiency. In the present investigation, as there are no bridging ligands between the photosensitizer and photocatalyst, electron transfer takes place through a through-space mechanism, typically characterized by a large decay coefficient factor \((\approx\!3\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1})\).[67] In other words, the efficiency of electron transfer is highly dependent on the molecular binding orientation between the electron-donor (photosensitizer) and electron-acceptor (photocatalyst). This study reveals that the incorporation of fluorinated linkers into the photocatalyst stabilizes the \([\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}]^{2+}\) complex photosensitizer through bidentate hydrogen bonding, thereby strengthening the intermolecular interaction and facilitating through-space electron transfer between the photosensitizer and photocatalyst. In the third step, when \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\)
molecules interact with the active sites (trinuclear oxo-centered Fe-O clusters[45,68]) on the MIL-101(Fe) photocatalyst, electrons are transferred to \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), leading to the formation of CO product and the regeneration of the photocatalyst. This study underscores the significance of deliberate ligand structure engineering for the development of MOF-based catalytic systems. However, it is crucial to acknowledge the necessity for comprehensive spectroscopic and microscopic investigations, in particular, under operational conditions, to gain a deeper understanding of the intermolecular interactions associated with these novel fluorinated MOFs.
## 4. Conclusion
In the present work, a facile strategy for enhancing the catalytic activity of MIL-101(Fe) in photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction through ligand structure engineering has been proposed. By incorporating a precisely controlled number of fluorinated linkers, A significant enhancement in the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\)-to-CO conversion rate was achieved. Combining comprehensive experimental characterizations and measurements with theoretical calculations, we have elucidated that the presence of fluorinated linkers in MIL-101(Fe) effectively stabilizes the Ru complex photosensitizer through hydrogen bonding interaction, resulting in a remarkable enhancement of intermolecular electron transfer kinetics between the photosensitizer and photocatalysts, a long-standing challenge in the field. Most importantly, this strategy can be extended to other Fe-MOFs, including MIL-53(Fe) and MIL-88(Fe), demonstrating its broad applicability in enhancing the photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction reaction in sensitized Fe-MOFs. Our findings not only offer a practical avenue to enhance the photocatalytic performance of Fe-MOFs but also demonstrate significant prospects for the systematic design of advanced photocatalytic systems with superior performance. This study also opens up new opportunities for the rational engineering of high-performance photocatalysts beyond Fe-MOFs, enabling the development of efficient and sustainable catalytic systems for various applications.
## 5. Experimental Section
Reagents: Iron(III) chloride hexahydrate \((\mathsf{F e C l}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O},\ 97\%)\), 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid \((\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C},\ 98\%)\), tetra-n-butylammonium hexafluorophosphate \((n\mathrm{-}\mathsf{B u})_{2}\mathsf{N}(\mathsf{P F}_{6})\), \(98\%\), and Nafion solution were purchased from Sigma–Aldrich. 2-fluoroterephthalic acid \((\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}-\) F, \(95\%\), isopropyl alcohol \((99\%)\), and sodium sulfate anhydrous \((\mathsf{N}_{2}\cdot\mathsf{S O}_{4}\), \(99\%\)) were purchased from Aladdin Industrial Corporation. 2,5-fluoroterephthalic acid \((\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}\!-\!\mathsf{F},\ 97\%)\) was purchased from Bidepharm. N,N'-dimethylformamide (DMF, \(99.8\%\)), acetone \((99.5\%)\), acetonitrile \((\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{C N},\ 99.8\%)\), and triethanolamine (TEOA, \(99\%\)) were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. All of the chemicals and reagents used in this study are analytical grade without additional purification. The deionized water was purified from Milli-Q System and used in all experiments.
Preparation of Fe-MOFs: Preparation of MIL-101(Fe): The pristine MIL101(Fe) was prepared by a hydrothermal method based on the previously reported procedure.[34] In a typical process, \(\mathsf{F e C l}_{3}\cdot6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) \((2.5\mathrm{\mmol})\) and \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}\) (1.25 mmol) were dissolved in \(30~\mathsf{m L}\) DMF solution and stirred magnetically at room temperature to form a uniform solution. Then, the above mixed solution was transferred to a \(50~\mathsf{m L}\) Teflon-lined stainlesssteel autoclave and heated to \(110\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24~\mathrm{h}\). After cooling, the products
were collected by centrifugation and were soaked in DMF for 3 days, with the solution replaced with fresh DMF \((20\,\mathsf{n L})\) every \(24~\mathrm{h}\) to eliminate the unreacted compound \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}\). After 3 days of soaking, the collected powder was further washed with acetone three times to remove the residual DMF. Finally, the orange powder was obtained after drying under a vacuum overnight at \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).
Preparation of xMIL-101-F \((x=0.125\), 0.25 and 0.5): The partially fluorinated MIL-101(Fe) denoted as xMIL-101-F \((x=0.125\), 0.25, and 0.5) were obtained via a mixed-ligand strategy using the same procedure as that for pristine MIL-101(Fe), where \(x\) represents the molar ratio of \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}\) - \(\mathsf{F}\mathrm{:}\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}\). The introduction amount of \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}\) -F is 0.156, 0.3125, and 0.625 mmol for 0.125MIL-101-F, 0.25MIL-101-F, and 0.5MIL-101-F, respectively.
Preparation of xMIL-101-F,F \((x=0.125,\,0.25\) and 0.5): The partially fluorinated MIL-101(Fe) denoted as xMIL-101-F, \((x=0.125,\,0.25\) , and 0.5) were obtained via a mixed-ligand strategy using the same procedure as that for pristine MIL-101(Fe), where \(x\) represents the molar ratio of \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}\) - \(\mathsf{F}\mathrm{:}\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}\) . The introduction amount of \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}\) -F is 0.156, 0.3125, and 0.625 mmol for 0.125MIL-101-F, 0.25MIL-101-F, and 0.5MIL-101-F, respectively.
Preparation of FeBDC-F and FeBDC-F,F: The perfluorinated Fe-MOFs denoted as FeBDC-F and FeBDC-F,F were obtained using the same procedure as that for pristine MIL-101(Fe), except that the \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}\) is replaced by \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}\) -F or \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}\) -F,F while other conditions remain unchanged.
Preparation of MIL-53(Fe): The pristine MIL-53(Fe) was prepared by a hydrothermal method based on the previously reported procedure.[69] In a typical process, \(\mathsf{F e C l}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) \((0.675\;\mathrm{mmol})\) and \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}\) (1 mmol) were dissolved in \(10\;\mathrm{mL}\) DMF solution with \(2\;\mathrm{mL}\) NaOH \((0.4\;\mathsf{m o l}\;\mathsf{L}^{-1})\) and stirred magnetically at room temperature to uniform solution. Then, the above mixed solution was transferred to a \(50~\mathrm{mL}\) Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave and heated at \(140~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(20\,\mathrm{h}\). After cooling, the products were collected by centrifugation, and washed with ethanol and water three times, respectively. Finally, the orange powder was obtained after drying under a vacuum overnight at \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).
Preparation of 0.25MIL-53-F,F: The partially fluorinated MIL-53(Fe) denoted as 0.25MIL-53-F,F was obtained via a mixed-ligand strategy using the same procedure as that for pristine MIL-53(Fe). The introduction amounts of \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}\) and \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}\) -F,F are 1.25 and 0.3125 mmol, respectively.
Preparation of MIL-88(Fe): The pristine MIL-88(Fe) was prepared by a hydrothermal method based on the previously reported procedure.[70] In a typical process, \(\mathsf{F e C l}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) \((0.675\;\mathrm{mmol})\) and \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}\) (1 mmol) were dissolved in \(10\;\mathrm{mL}\) DMF solution with \(2\;\mathrm{mL}\) NaOH \((0.4\;\mathsf{m o l}\;\mathsf{L}^{-1})\) and stirred magnetically at room temperature to uniform solution. Then, the above mixed solution was transferred to a \(50~\mathrm{mL}\) Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave and heated at \(100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(15~\mathrm{h}\). After natural cooling, the products were collected by centrifugation, and washed with DMF and methanol three times, respectively. Finally, the orange powder was obtained after drying under a vacuum overnight at \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).
In all lighting experiments, a 300 W xenon lamp equipped with a \(420\;\mathsf{nm}\) UV cut-off filter was used as a light source. XPS measurements were performed on Thermofisher K-alpha spectroscopy using monochromatized Al Ka at \(\mathsf{h v}=1486.6\;\mathsf{e V}\). The binding energies were referenced to the adventitious C 1s peak at \(284.8\;\mathsf{e V}\). EPR tests were conducted on a Bruker A300 spectrometer. The steady-state photoluminescence (PL) measurements were carried out on a Hitachi-F2500 fluorescence spectrophotometer excited at \(360\;\mathsf{nm}\). Time-resolved photoluminescence spectroscopy was collected on the XH2162 fluorescence lifetime testing system with an excitation wavelength of \(375\;\mathsf{nm}\) and detection wavelength of \(450\;\mathsf{nm}\). Fe K-edge XAFS measurements were performed at BL14W1 station in Shanghai Synchrotron Radiation Facility (SSRF), using a double-crystal Si (111) monochromator. The averaging of SSRF was operated at \(2.5\;\mathsf{GeV}\) with a current of \(300\;\mathsf{mA}\). Data were acquired in fluorescence excitation mode using a Lytle detector at room temperature. The XAFS data were background subtracted, normalized, and Fourier transformed by standard procedures within the ATHENA program. Morlet wavelet transform of EXAFS data was processed by the HAMA Fortan program.
Photocatalytic \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) Reduction Measurements: The photocatalytic reduction of \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) to CO was conducted under 1 atm of ultrapure \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) \((>\!99.999\%)\) atmosphere at \(25\;\mathsf{^\circ C}\) in a \(250\;\mathsf{m L}\) quartz reaction cell containing \(50\;\mathsf{m g}\) photocatalyst, \(50\;\mathsf{m g}\) \(\left[\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}\mathsf{C l}_{2}\cdot6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O},\right.\) \(50\;\mathsf{m L}\) \(\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{C N}\), and \(10\;\mathsf{m L}\) TEOA. Prior to each experiment, \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) was bubbled through the solution for \(30\;\mathsf{m i n}\) to eliminate the dissolved \(\mathsf{O}_{2}\). Subsequently, the solution was exposed to irradiation from a \(300\;\mathsf{W}\) Xe lamp equipped with a \(420\;\mathsf{nm}\) UV cut-off filter for a duration of \(6\;\mathsf{h}\). The gaseous products generated during the photocatalytic \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) reduction were sampled and quantified using a gas chromatograph (GC, Agilent 8890) with both thermal conductivity (TCD) and flame ionization (FID) detectors. Potential liquid-phase products were analyzed through \(^{1}\mathsf{H}\)-NMR and high-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC). Each photocatalytic reaction was conducted three times to ensure data reliability. Recycling experiments involved the retrieval of previously used catalysts from parallel tests, followed by rinsing and vacuum drying before reuse in subsequent 6-h runs.
Computational Details: Density functional theory (DFT) calculations were carried out to investigate the \(\mathsf{H}-\mathsf{F}\) hydrogen bonding interaction between Ru and the Fe-MOF model system. The cluster models consisted of Fe(III) \((\upmu_{3}-\mathsf{O})\) metal nodes coordinated to six benzoates as illustrated in the MIL-101 framework (Figure S24, Supporting Information), which represented a single secondary building unit (SBU) connection for MIL-101.[26,71] All DFT calculations were performed at the B3LYP level with the 6–31G (d, p) basis set for the elements of C, H, O, N, and the LANL2DZ basis set for Ru and Fe, which is implemented in the Gaussian 16 program.[72] The DFT-D3 scheme was employed to account for van der Waals (vdW) interactions using an empirical damped potential term added to the electronic energy.[73] The binding energy (BE) was calculated as \(\mathsf{B E}=\mathsf{E}(\mathsf{c o m p l e x})-\mathsf{E}(\mathsf{R u})-\mathsf{E}(\mathsf{M O F})\), whereas E(complex), E(Ru), and E(MOF) are the electronic energies of Ru-MOF complex, Ru and MOF, respectively.[74]
